+# Study 01: does the complexity score predict where 30 m and finer results differ?
+
+**Status: in progress.** The plan below was committed before any 10 m or 20 m row was scored. The results are added after it.
+
+**Question** (plan section 15c, the next step the desk analysis named; contract 0.10.0 section 4.4). The quality map of the
+[topic page](../README.md) scores every 30 m cell for how complex its terrain is (steepness, cliffs, gullies, landform, 10 m relief), a first definition that was
+never tuned and never checked against an error measure. A fire mesh that is finer only where that score is high would pay off only if the 30 m and the finer answers
+differ mostly where the score is high. This study runs the same scenario at 30, 20 and 10 m and measures that.
+
+**What this does not do.** It builds no refinement and changes nothing under `src/`. It measures one thing: where the arrival times of two grids differ and whether the
+score points there. It does not say that the finer grid is right (that is the validity work, 15d), and it cannot say how much of a difference a local refinement would remove.
+
+## Plan (fixed before the 10 m and 20 m rows were scored)
+
+**Runs.** The standard scenario of the quality map (`scripts/01_baseline.run.ts`): a 6 km square of a bundled site, the preset "hot NW wind ahead of a SW change" from its canonical
+11:00, the fast tier, one point ignition of 30 m radius at t = 0 (the same point at every cell size: the flammable cell of the 30 m grid with slope under 10 degrees nearest to 1500 m west and
+1500 m north of the centre), 8 simulated hours, seed 1. Fire cells of 30, 20 and 10 m (`scripts/10_ladder.row.ts`; 10 m and 20 m by the same in-memory patch of the builder's
+high-detail cell that the desktop-simulation study 01 uses, `scripts/10_ladder.launch.mjs`; `src/` is not changed). Two settings of the embers: **on** (the standard scenario as the quality map ran it) and **off**
+(a control: with embers on, a change of cell size also changes the random draws, so a difference mixes the grid and chance; with embers off the differences are the grid alone). Both are reported, and the choice
+between them is not made after looking. A second seed at 30 m with embers on gives the size of the difference that chance alone makes. Six sites, one realisation per row.
+
+**Sites and the hold-out.** Development sites, the only ones on which anything may be chosen: **Katoomba, Thredbo, Warrumbungles**. Hold-out sites, scored once after the choices are frozen: **Blackheath (Grose Valley, `grose`), Kanangra Walls, Mount Tomah**.
+The complexity score as defined (the plain mean of the five groups) was never fitted to any run, so it is scored as it is on every site. Anything that is changed, here only a re-weighting of the five groups fitted on the three
+development sites, is scored on the hold-out sites only. This script was committed before the hold-out sites were scored.
+
+**The difference.** For a cell, D = the absolute difference in minutes between the arrival times of the two grids, each capped at the horizon H (a cell the front has not reached by H counts as H, so a cell burnt in one run and not in the
+other has a difference of at least the time left). The main case: the 30 m grid, the 30 m run against the 10 m run (a 30 m cell holds 3 x 3 cells of 10 m: the mean of their capped arrival times), H = 8 h. Scored cells: cells that can burn in the 30 m model
+and that either run reaches by H. Other cases: H = 4 h (about the time the wind change arrives); 60 m blocks for 30 m against 20 m, 20 m against 10 m and 30 m against 10 m (20 m cells do not tile the 30 m grid).
+**The local difference** E: the size of the gradient of the signed difference over the 30 m cells (central differences, minutes per cell), over cells that both runs reach fully with all four neighbours too. A difference piles up along the
+fire's path, so D says where a gap has grown and E where it was made.
+
+**The predictors.** The score as defined and each of its five groups alone (S steepness, C cliff, G gully, L landform, R 10 m relief); two baselines that carry no terrain complexity: the 30 m slope, and the arrival time itself (gaps grow with time).
+
+**The measures** (per site, per embers setting): (1) Spearman rank correlation of the score with D over the scored cells, and the same with the arrival time held fixed; (2) the **share of the summed difference that the top-20 % set carries**
+(the 20 % of the whole domain with the highest score, as the quality map defines it), with the share the cells of the set would carry by size alone, and the most any set of that size could carry; (3) against **random sets of the same size**, against random sets
+with the same number of cells in each tenth of the arrival-time range, and against **the same set moved elsewhere** on the map (the eight symmetries of the square and wrap-around shifts, which keep the set's shape: complex cells and large differences both come in
+patches, so scattered random sets are too easy a comparison); (4) recall of the cells with the largest D (top 20 % of the scored cells) by the top-20 % set. Draws: 2000 random, 1000 time-matched, 1000 moved, 400 for the correlation; seeded.
+
+**The tuned version.** Weights of the five groups on a grid of step 0.1 (1001 vectors), chosen to maximise the mean rank correlation with D over the development sites (30 m against 10 m, 8 h, both embers settings); its numbers on the hold-out sites are reported next to the score as defined.
+The best single group is chosen the same way.
+
+**What would count as a positive result** (written before the runs): on the hold-out sites the top-20 % set carries clearly more of the difference than the best random set of its size and than the set moved elsewhere, in both embers settings, with a lift well above 1 (a lift of 1 is "no better than chance by size"),
+and the rank correlation is positive on every hold-out site. **A negative result** is a lift near 1, or a correlation that is gone once the arrival time is held fixed, or one that holds in one embers setting only.
+
+## Files
+
+Scripts (all in `../scripts/`): `10_ladder.launch.mjs` and `10_ladder.row.ts` (one row), `11_ladder.batch.mjs` (rows one after the other under the machine's lock), `12_aggregate.run.ts` (full rows to one small container per site),
+`13_score.run.ts` (the measures), `lib/ladderio.ts` and `lib/stats.ts`.
